## Unnecessary Re-renders in React

---

### 1. Reference Equality

React uses `Object.is` to compare props. Primitives compare by value; objects and functions compare by **reference**.

```jsx
// New object created every render → child always re-renders
<Child config={{ theme: 'dark' }} />

// New function created every render → same problem
<Child onClick={() => doSomething()} />
```

Every render of the parent creates a *new* reference, even if the value is logically identical. React sees a different prop and re-renders the child.

---

### 2. The Three Tools

| Hook | Memoizes | Use when |
|------|----------|----------|
| `React.memo` | A **component** | Child is pure and re-renders with same props |
| `useMemo` | A **computed value** | Expensive calculation; value passed as prop |
| `useCallback` | A **function reference** | Function passed as prop to a memoized child |

**`useCallback` without `React.memo` on the child is useless** — stabilizing the reference only matters if the child actually bails out on equal props.

```jsx
// useMemo: expensive calculation
const sorted = useMemo(() => heavySort(items), [items]);

// useCallback: stable function reference
const handleClick = useCallback((id) => dispatch(remove(id)), [dispatch]);
```

Only memoize when there's a measurable cost. Memoization itself has overhead.

---

### 3. Context Provider Pitfalls

Every consumer re-renders when the context **value reference** changes — even if the data is the same.

```jsx
// BAD: new object every render
function Provider({ children }) {
  const [user, setUser] = useState(null);
  return (
    <UserContext.Provider value={{ user, setUser }}>
      {children}
    </UserContext.Provider>
  );
}
```

The `{ user, setUser }` object literal is recreated on every render. Fix it with `useMemo`:

```jsx
// GOOD: stable reference unless user changes
function Provider({ children }) {
  const [user, setUser] = useState(null);
  const value = useMemo(() => ({ user, setUser }), [user]);
  return (
    <UserContext.Provider value={value}>
      {children}
    </UserContext.Provider>
  );
}
```

A deeper fix: **split read and write contexts** so components that only call `setUser` never re-render when `user` changes.

---

### 4. Before / After

**Before** — `List` re-renders on every keystroke in the parent's input:

```jsx
function Parent() {
  const [query, setQuery] = useState('');
  const [items, setItems] = useState([...]);

  const filtered = items.filter(i => i.active);   // new array every render
  const handleRemove = (id) => setItems(prev =>    // new function every render
    prev.filter(i => i.id !== id)
  );

  return (
    <>
      <input value={query} onChange={e => setQuery(e.target.value)} />
      <List items={filtered} onRemove={handleRemove} />
    </>
  );
}

function List({ items, onRemove }) { /* ... */ }
```

**After** — `List` only re-renders when `items` actually changes:

```jsx
function Parent() {
  const [query, setQuery] = useState('');
  const [items, setItems] = useState([...]);

  const filtered = useMemo(
    () => items.filter(i => i.active),
    [items]                                        // stable unless items changes
  );

  const handleRemove = useCallback(
    (id) => setItems(prev => prev.filter(i => i.id !== id)),
    []                                             // stable forever
  );

  return (
    <>
      <input value={query} onChange={e => setQuery(e.target.value)} />
      <List items={filtered} onRemove={handleRemove} />
    </>
  );
}

const List = React.memo(function List({ items, onRemove }) { /* ... */ });
//    ^^^^ bails out when props are referentially equal
```

The `query` state changes on every keystroke, but `List` now skips re-rendering because `filtered` and `handleRemove` references are stable.

---

**Rule of thumb:** profile first with React DevTools Profiler, then memoize only the components that show up as expensive or frequently re-rendering.
